[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-18
TITLE: Grid control clock - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware shall generate a stable grid control clock within the frequency
range specified by the SRS and shall leave the grid-clock output inactive when
the clock is disabled.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and ready to use.
* A treatment plan should be ready to load.
* An oscilloscope or logic analyzer is connected to the grid-clock output.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Power on the system.
2. Load a treatment plan.
3. Observe the grid-clock output with the measurement instrument.
4. Measure the grid-clock frequency.
5. Disable the grid clock using the approved service or test control.
6. Observe the grid-clock output after disabling it.
7. Re-enable the grid clock and observe the output.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the system is powered on successfully.
2. Verify the plan is loaded successfully.
3. Verify the grid-clock output is present when enabled.
4. Verify the measured frequency is between 1 kHz and 20 kHz.
5. Verify the clock stops when disabled.
6. Verify the output remains inactive after disabling the clock.
7. Verify the clock restarts cleanly when re-enabled.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-1
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-19
TITLE: Elapsed-time high-voltage regulation - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
Verify that high-voltage closed-loop commands use elapsed-time ramp rates, bounded output, feedback
stability timing, and feedback-discrepancy cancellation.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on with high-voltage control unlocked and emission inactive.
* An HVPS test fixture can control high-voltage feedback independently of the requested setpoint.
* An approved diagnostic trace can capture requested voltage, feedback, DAC command,
  high-voltage-ramping status, and timestamps with at most 1 ms resolution.
* The default high-voltage configuration values are loaded.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Request increasing and decreasing high-voltage targets while using the fixture to produce
   absolute feedback errors below 5 kV, from 5 kV through less than 20 kV, and at least 20 kV.
2. Capture consecutive high-voltage commands and their timestamps in each error band.
3. Drive feedback through the requested target, then supply samples within +/-2% interspersed with
   samples outside that tolerance, and observe the high-voltage-ramping status immediately before
   and after 4000 ms of accumulated in-tolerance sample time.
4. Request values that exercise the lower and configured 100 kV upper command limits.
5. During an increasing ramp, hold feedback so the command is at least 50 kV and exceeds feedback by
   more than 50 kV.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the firmware evaluates the ramp on the 5 ms feedback-control cadence in both directions.
2. Verify the default command changes by 0.025 kV per cycle below 5 kV error and 0.1 kV per cycle in
   both larger error bands, corresponding to 5 kV/s and 20 kV/s elapsed-time rates.
3. Verify only samples within +/-2% add 5 ms to stability time, the ramping status remains set
   before the accumulator reaches 4000 ms, and the status clears on the first sample at or above
   that threshold.
4. Verify the command never falls below 0 kV or exceeds the configured 100 kV maximum.
5. Verify the feedback-discrepancy condition sets the command to zero and clears the ramping status.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-8
  ROLE: Verifies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-11
  ROLE: Verifies
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Verifies

