[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-30
TITLE: Emission-current grid regulation - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
Verify the 5 ms proportional-integral grid controller, including requested-voltage gain selection,
the current-error hold band, asymmetric slew limits, output bounds, anti-windup, and controller
activation and reset behavior.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and ready to use.
* A treatment plan is ready to load and grid control is enabled.
* A fixture can control cathode-current feedback above and below the requested current.
* An approved diagnostic trace can capture requested power and voltage, requested and measured
  cathode current, proportional gain, integral state, grid command, DAC writes, and timestamps with
  at most 1 ms resolution.
* Instruments can observe the four external DAC chip-select signals.
* The default grid-controller configuration values are loaded.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Power on the system and observe the four DAC chip-select signals after initialization and while
   no DAC transaction is active.
2. Load a plan and record the requested power, requested high voltage, derived cathode-current
   target, and default grid-controller configuration.
3. Start emission and capture the initial grid command.
4. At requested voltages at or below 50 kV, above 50 kV through 70 kV, and above 70 kV, apply
   positive and negative cathode-current errors greater than 1% and capture consecutive commands.
5. After moving the output away from its maximum, reverse the error to exercise both decreasing and
   increasing command slew.
6. Apply errors within +/-1% of the requested current and observe the grid command and DAC writes.
7. Drive the calculated command to each output or slew limit, then reverse the error and observe
   integral recovery.
8. During active control, exchange main-control commands and observe I/O, ADC, DAC, and inactive DAC
   chip-select signals between transactions.
9. End emission or disable grid control, observe the command, and then start emission again.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify all four DAC chip-select signals initialize inactive high and remain high while idle.
2. Verify requested cathode current equals requested power divided by requested high voltage and the
   defaults are: gains -25, -35, and -150; integral time 0.2 s; downward slew 3000 units/s; upward
   slew 1000 units/s; and output limits 50 to 600 units.
3. Verify activation clears controller history and writes the 600-unit maximum-grid command.
4. Verify 5 ms calculations use the gain for each requested-voltage band and apply the integral
   increment `(Kp / 0.2 s) * 0.005 s * error`.
5. Verify consecutive commands decrease by no more than 15 units and increase by no more than
   5 units per 5 ms cycle.
6. Verify no grid DAC command is issued while the absolute current error is within 1% of target.
7. Verify the command remains from 50 through 600 units and the integral changes only when the
   candidate is reachable or unwinds saturation.
8. Verify communication, I/O, and ADC/DAC servicing continues during regulation and all inactive DAC
   chip-select signals remain high between transactions.
9. Verify disabling control restores 600 units and clears controller history so reactivation starts
   from 600 units.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-14
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-31
TITLE: FTDI bootloader entry - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
A valid FTDI bootloader request shall cause the application firmware to reset into the bootloader.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and running the application firmware.
* A USB FTDI host tool that can transmit the seven-byte bootloader frame is connected to USART3.
* An approved method is available to observe the system reset and confirm that the bootloader is active.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Confirm that the application firmware is running and responding normally.
2. Send the seven-byte `*B00TL\n` frame over the FTDI interface.
3. Observe the reset and interrogate the bootloader with the approved host tool.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the application is operational before the request.
2. Verify the application resets after receiving the valid bootloader frame.
3. Verify the bootloader becomes active and accepts the approved host interaction.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-19
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-32
TITLE: Bootloader request CRC-page erasure - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
Before resetting into the bootloader, a valid FTDI bootloader request shall erase the application CRC
flash page without erasing adjacent pages.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and running the application firmware.
* A USB FTDI host tool is connected to USART3.
* An approved flash-memory inspection method is available, and the application CRC page contains known
  non-erased data.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Record the contents of the application CRC page and its adjacent flash pages.
2. Send the seven-byte `*B00TL\n` frame over the FTDI interface.
3. After the reset enters the bootloader, inspect the same flash pages before restarting the application.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the CRC page contains the known data and retain the adjacent-page contents for comparison.
2. Verify the system resets after receiving the valid bootloader frame.
3. Verify the complete CRC page is erased to `0xFF` and the adjacent pages are unchanged.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-19
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-38
TITLE: PC Communication 3 - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
When the system is powered on, the user can retrieve or update an individual configuration
parameter through the FTDI GET and SET protocol. Binary payload bytes do not end a frame early, and
the receiver remains available after traffic arrives while a complete frame awaits processing.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and ready to use.
* A USB FTDI host tool that can transmit and capture binary frames is connected to the HVPS board.
* A valid configuration index and an approved value whose little-endian float encoding contains a
  `0x0A` payload byte are available.
* An approved FTDI timing harness can delay deferred command processing while UART receive
  interrupts continue.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Power on the system and begin capturing FTDI traffic.
2. Send an 11-byte GET frame containing `*GET..`, configuration category 0 as a little-endian
   16-bit value, the valid index as a little-endian 16-bit value, and a newline terminator.
3. Record the returned value and response bytes.
4. Send a 15-byte SET frame containing `*..SET`, the same category and index fields, the approved
   value containing the `0x0A` payload byte, and a newline terminator.
5. Record the SET response, then repeat the GET request and record its response.
6. Use the timing harness to deliver one additional byte after a complete valid GET frame while
   deferred processing of that frame remains pending. Release processing, wait for it to complete,
   then send and capture another valid GET request.
7. Send separate requests with an unrecognized command header, an unsupported category, a SET index
   outside the configuration table, and an index of 64 or greater.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the valid GET returns a 15-byte frame containing `*GET..`, the requested category and
   index, the selected value as a little-endian IEEE-754 32-bit float, and a newline terminator.
2. Verify the `0x0A` payload byte does not terminate the SET frame early. The SET updates the
   selected configuration parameter and returns the corresponding 15-byte `*..SET` confirmation.
3. Verify the repeated GET returns the updated value.
4. Verify the additional byte received while processing is pending is discarded without stalling
   reception, and the final valid GET returns the expected response.
5. Verify each unsupported request is ignored without a response and does not change the selected
   configuration parameter.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-19
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-39
TITLE: PC Communication 4 - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
When the system is powered on, the user can retrieve every configuration value, setpoint, or
feedback value with the corresponding FTDI bulk-read command.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and ready to use.
* A USB FTDI host tool that can transmit and capture binary frames is connected to the HVPS board.
* The expected 32 configuration values and six setpoints are available by index.
* Stable feedback inputs are applied and the expected five feedback values are independently
  observable.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Power on the system and begin capturing FTDI traffic.
2. Send the seven-byte `*ACFGS\n` request and capture the complete response.
3. Send the seven-byte `*ASPTS\n` request and capture the complete response.
4. Record the five stable feedback values, send the seven-byte `*AFBVS\n` request, and capture the
   complete response.
5. Decode the bytes after each response's six-byte `*` and command header as consecutive
   little-endian IEEE-754 32-bit floats through the final newline.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify `ACFGS` produces exactly one 135-byte response beginning with `*ACFGS`, followed by all 32
   configuration values in increasing index order, and ending with a newline.
2. Verify `ASPTS` produces exactly one 31-byte response beginning with `*ASPTS`, followed by all six
   setpoints in increasing index order, and ending with a newline.
3. Verify `AFBVS` produces exactly one 27-byte response beginning with `*AFBVS`, followed by all five
   feedback values in increasing index order, and ending with a newline.
4. Verify every decoded value matches the corresponding expected value for its category and index.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWHVPS-19
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-33
TITLE: Elapsed-time filament-current ramping - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
Verify the filament command's elapsed-time ramp rate, output limits, feedback stability time, and
command-to-feedback discrepancy response during warmup.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and ready to use.
* A treatment plan is ready to load.
* An HVPS test fixture can control filament-current feedback independently of the requested current.
* An approved diagnostic trace can capture requested current, feedback, filament command, warming
  status, and timestamps with at most 1 ms resolution.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Load the treatment plan and begin capturing the filament command and feedback during warmup.
2. Keep feedback below the target and capture consecutive command updates.
3. Supply feedback from the target through 2% above it, interspersed with feedback more than 2%
   above the target, and observe warming status around 4000 ms of accumulated qualifying sample
   time.
4. Start another warmup and, after the command reaches the configured minimum, hold feedback more
   than 300 mA below the command around 6000 ms of accumulated qualifying sample time.
5. Request a filament target below the configured minimum.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify warmup starts and the filament command remains within the configured limits.
2. Verify the command increases by 2.25 mA every 3 ms, corresponding to 750 mA/s.
3. Verify only samples from the target through 2% above it add 3 ms to stability time, warming
   remains active before the accumulator reaches 4000 ms, and warming clears on the first sample at
   or above that threshold.
4. Verify the command and warming status remain active before 6000 ms of qualifying discrepancy
   samples, then the command becomes zero and warming clears at the threshold.
5. Verify a target below the configured minimum sets the target and filament command to zero and clears warming.
<<<
