[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWHVPS-35
TITLE: External ADC feedback acquisition - Test Case
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware shall acquire high-voltage and cathode-current feedback as 16-sample paired bursts
every 5 ms, convert each valid channel average to engineering units, and publish the paired result.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
* The system is powered on and ready to use.
* An external signal source or suitable HVPS test fixture with accuracy appropriate for the
  approved measurement tolerance is connected.
* A logic analyzer or oscilloscope can observe both external ADC interfaces.
* The feedback display or approved diagnostic trace is available.
* Main-control communication, I/O activity, and external DAC transfers can be observed.
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Power on the system.
2. Observe both external ADC chip-select signals while acquisition is idle.
3. Capture consecutive complete feedback acquisition sequences.
4. For each burst, measure the SPI clock rate, count the paired samples, and measure the burst
   interval and completion time.
5. Record the 16 raw samples and published result for each feedback channel.
6. While repeated bursts and feedback-control processing run, send main-control commands, exercise
   I/O, and issue DAC output changes.
7. Repeat with known input values across the approved operating range.
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the system starts successfully.
2. Verify both ADC chip-select signals are inactive while idle.
3. Verify both ADC channels are acquired through nonblocking DMA transfers.
4. Verify both SPI clocks operate at 2.25 Mbit/s and each burst contains 16 paired samples, starts
   every 5 ms, and completes within 2 ms.
5. Verify each published engineering-unit feedback value corresponds to its channel's averaged
   16-bit code using the 4.096 V reference and documented channel scaling.
6. Verify communication, I/O, and DAC requests continue to be serviced within approved timing
   limits without repeated ADC acquisition-timing failures.
7. Verify the published values are within the approved measurement tolerance.
<<<
